paper/main.tex:70:\paragraph{Baselines.} B2 posts prices for three claim tiers, allocates by those prices, and audits uniformly. B3 allocates each job, in descending order of value, to the offer with remaining capacity and the highest nonnegative score $v_j c_i - \mathrm{bid}_i$, pays $v_j c_i - s_{2,j}$ with $s_{2,j}$ the highest score among the other eligible offers (zero if there is none), and audits uniformly at random among served jobs, spread over the rounds. Both refund a failed job and ban its identity for $L$ rounds.
paper/main.tex:132:\paragraph{Search quality.} Scheduled cells get a doubling check: the registered search continues with as many restarts again, and the cell is flagged if the doubled search improves the attacker's normalised profit or the reference's normalised profit (profit cells), the welfare attack's efficiency (welfare cells), or the coalition objective by at least \const{0.005}, or if a W1 search finds a feasible candidate where the original found none. A doubled search's winner is reported beside the cell and never replaces its frozen estimate. Cells without a scheduled check carry no search-quality claim. A cell is \emph{missing} if it did not complete or is invalid: not run (a cap stop or a cut), a refused sample, a nonfinite statistic, a search with no finite candidate, or a failed load of H4; a missing cell removes every decision that uses it. A \emph{search-flagged} cell keeps its decision, labelled as a decision about the prescribed search procedure, and leaves that rule's robustness statement for that family unanswered. The search bounds exposure to the searched families and optimiser; it does not certify robustness.
paper/main.tex:191:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
paper/main.tex:222:\caption{Q4 per market: B3 minus R3 gain difference and its calibrated lower bound, R3's efficiency and lower bound, the raw one-sided bootstrap $p$-values of the two components, the calibrated market value $\max(p'_{\mathrm{gain}}, p'_{\mathrm{eff}})$ that enters Holm, its Holm-adjusted value, and the decision. The decisions on fresh0, fresh1, fresh3, and fresh4 are about the prescribed search procedure; the table marks the direct flags (fresh1, fresh4), and fresh0 and fresh3 depend on flagged cells through their references (addendum).}\label{tab:q4}
paper/main.tex:230:\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
paper/main.tex:247:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when its probability-weighted component gains satisfy that constraint; we examine such mixtures on the existing evaluation records. On the records used to select them, W0 weights of \site{r_ph_w_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_w_cs} for C$^*$, with the H4 attack as companion, give estimated normalised gains of \site{r_ph_g_r3} and \site{r_ph_g_cs} and gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} and \site{r_ph_e_cs}; with the public D2+H3 attack as companion the efficiencies are \site{r_ph_e_r3_d} and \site{r_ph_e_cs_d} (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). The registered W1 constraint is a non-negative mean gain on the training seeds. Reconstructed from the search archives, the training mean gains of these mixtures, each at its rule--companion pair's weight in Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}, are \site{r_ph_t_r3} and \site{r_ph_t_r3_d} for R3 and \site{r_ph_t_cs} and \site{r_ph_t_cs_d} for C$^*$ (H4 and D2+H3 companions): three meet the training constraint, but C$^*$'s public-family mixture does not. At its evaluation-selected weight of \site{r_ph_wd_cs}, C$^*$'s public-family mixture fails the training constraint. At \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, the largest weight on the same grid at which both the training mean gain and the evaluation bound are non-negative, it has training gain \site{r_ph_ft_cs}, estimated evaluation gain \site{r_ph_fg_cs}, and gross efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs}, against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (last row of Table~\ref{tab:ph-welfare}). Both rules therefore show substantial empirical efficiency losses with public-family mixtures alone; H4 is not needed for a drop of this size, and population profitability remains unestablished. The evaluation-selected weights are the largest at which a nominal one-sided \const{95} percent normal bound on the mean gain stays non-negative (the training-feasible row also requires a non-negative training mean); that bound is not adjusted for selection, the weight and the companion family were chosen on the evaluation seeds, and the bound is close to zero at the selected weights, so this checks the policy class and is not an out-of-sample estimate. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch